# As Americans grow older, how much of the day do they spend alone?
subtitle: "Draw how much of the day Americans spend alone from 15 to 80, then hear two hosts weigh friends, loneliness and the young."
tags: ["psychology","data","culture"]
format: quiz
thumb: "band \"How alone at 80?\" note \"draw your guess\""

## As Americans grow older, how much of the day do they spend alone?
use: line_chart
with:
    x:
      - 15
      - 20
      - 25
      - 30
      - 35
      - 40
      - 45
      - 50
      - 55
      - 60
      - 65
      - 70
      - 75
      - 80
    series:
      - {name: Alone, values: [3.7, 5.1, 5.2, 4.7, 4.7, 5.2, 5.5, 6.6, 6.7, 7.2, 7.6, 7.7, 7.8, 8]}
      - {name: Friends, values: [1.5, 1.8, 1.1, 0.8, 0.6, 0.5, 0.5, 0.4, 0.5, 0.5, 0.5, 0.6, 0.6, 0.5]}
    xlim:
      - 15
      - 80
    ylim:
      - 0
      - 9
    y_label: Hours a day
    x_label: Age
    label_size: 40
sources:
    - id: owid_time
      title: Who Americans spend their time with, by age
      authors: Our World in Data, from the US Bureau of Labor Statistics' American Time Use Survey
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/time-spent-with-relationships-by-age-us
      finding: 'averages for 2010–2024: about 3.7 hours a day alone at age 15, rising to about 8 hours at 80; time with friends about 1.5–1.8 hours at 15–20, about half an hour from age 40 on'
    - id: owid_article
      title: Who do Americans spend time with over their lives?
      authors: Tuna Acisu (Our World in Data)
      year: 2026
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/who-do-americans-spend-time-with-over-their-lives
      finding: as they get older, Americans spend more time alone, but surveys show this does not necessarily mean they are lonely
    - id: owid_young
      title: Young Americans spend much more time alone than they did fifteen years ago
      authors: Bastian Herre and Tuna Acisu (Our World in Data)
      year: 2025
      url: https://ourworldindata.org/data-insights/young-americans-spend-much-more-time-alone-than-they-did-fifteen-years-ago
      finding: Americans aged 15–29 spent about 4 hours a day alone in 2010 and about 6 in 2023, 45 % more

    dot p80 #b5482e at.data [80,8] hidden true
    text p80_l "8 hours" below p80 gap 30 #b5482e cites ["owid_time"] font_size 52 hidden true
    text half "30 min" #2f6b8f at.data [57,1.7] cites ["owid_time"] font_size 52 hidden true
    dot p15 #b5482e at.data [15,3.7] hidden true
    text p15_l "15: under 4 h" above-right p15 gap 20 #b5482e cites ["owid_time"] font_size 40 hidden true
    dot y4 #2f6b8f at.data [22,4] hidden true
    dot y6 #2f6b8f at.data [22,6] hidden true
    arrow yarrow y4 -> y6 #2f6b8f hidden true
    text young "15–29: 4 → 6 h" above-right y6 gap 24 #2f6b8f cites ["owid_young"] font_size 44 hidden true
    text since "2010 → 2023" above young gap 8 #5e584e font_size 34 hidden true
    text notlonely "alone ≠ lonely" #5e584e at.data [64,5.4] cites ["owid_article"] font_size 48 hidden true

    card title "Alone: more or less with age?"

A: As Americans grow older, how much of their day do they spend alone? At fifteen, it's under four hours.
    draw axes p15 p15_l

B: Easy. Then you get a partner, kids, colleagues. You're never alone again until retirement.
    highlight p15_l

    ask "Draw the line from 15 to 80." on line_1 from 15 store g
        right: Close. Your line ends at {g} hours; the real one ends near {g.true}.
        wrong: Your line ends at {g} hours; the real one ends near {g.true}.

    erase p15_l

A: It climbs almost all the way. Mos (@draw p80 p80_l@)t of the waking day, spent alone.

B: Eight hours? That's a full working day in my own company.
    highlight p80_l

A: And friends go the other way. Teenagers get about an hour and a half a day with friends.
    draw line_2

A: From forty on, it's about half an hour.
    draw half

B: Half an hour. That's one coffee. A short one.
    highlight line_2

A: But alone isn't the same as lonely. S (@draw notlonely@)urveys show the extra time alone doesn't necessarily make older people feel lonelier.

    erase notlonely half

A: The bigger change is at the young end. Americans aged fiftee (@draw y4 y6 yarrow young since@)n to twenty-nine spent about four hours a day alone in 2010. By 2023, six.

B: So my teenage nephew is training early for retirement.
    highlight young

